# KNOWN GAP — a MODULE-LEVEL global's initializer is not type-checked at all,
# and the two backends then DISAGREE about what to do with the garbage.
#
# `int zz = "not an int"` at module level:
#     gg check                  -> "OK: no semantic errors"   (rc 0, no diagnostic)
#     gg build (C)              -> rc 1, a raw gcc error reaching the user
#     gg build --backend=llvm   -> rc 0, BUILDS
#     ./the-binary (LLVM)       -> rc 0, prints garbage (e.g. 187650881467040)
#
# Measured at HEAD (2026-08-17).
#
# ⚠ THE SAME MISMATCH AS A LOCAL IS CORRECTLY REJECTED:
#     void main():
#         int zz = "not an int"      -> error[E_TypeMismatch]
# So the check EXISTS and is simply not called at the module-level position.
# That makes this a missing call at one write site, not a missing capability —
# the same shape as the filed undefined-type-name hole, and a Core #4
# chokepoint fix rather than a new analysis.
#
# THREE defects by Core #8, from one accepted program:
#   (1) a program that must be REJECTED is accepted at check time;
#   (2) the C lane surfaces a raw `.c:` compiler error to the user, which
#       `docs/language-design.md:120` already forbids;
#   (3) the LLVM lane SILENTLY MISCOMPILES it — builds, runs, prints garbage.
#       "Both backends are wrong in different ways" is two bugs, not one, and
#       the silent one is the worse of the two.
#
# ⚠ DISTINCT from the filed SELF-HOST entries on the same syntax. Those record
# `gg-selfhost` accepting `int x = "s"`; this is RUST gg, where the local form
# is correctly rejected and only the module-level position leaks. Do not merge
# them: different lane, different write site.
#
# INTENDED: `gg check` REJECTS this with `E_TypeMismatch`, exactly as it does
# for the local form, on both lanes.
#
# Wired test: known_gap_global_initializer_type_mismatch_rejected
# (tests/integration.rs, #[ignore]d). It asserts the REJECTION, so it fails
# today and goes green when the module-level position is checked.
int zz = "not an int"

void main():
    print(zz)
